CoreMethod::is_protected is the same dead-field shape as is_const was, and nobody has checked whether any typedb dump populates it


## Flagged by lane-enum while fixing B125, 2026-09-01, deliberately not widened into that ticket

`CoreMethod::is_protected` is **the same shape as the field B125 just fixed**: it
exists, greps cleanly, type-checks, and nobody has checked whether a single
typedb dump populates it. If it does not, `#[serde(default)]` makes it silently
answer `false` to every caller.

The lane did not check, and said so rather than guessing — the right call, since
widening a ticket on a hunch is how measured work turns into assumed work.

## This is a one-line measurement

Count how many of the 1002 Core class methods carry a `protected` key in the
dumps. Then:

- **If populated:** confirm the loader reads it and something consumes it. A
  populated field with no reader is B125's other half.
- **If not populated:** either populate it (check whether the `decl` string
  carries the information, the way ` const` turned out to) or delete it, and pin
  the decision with a test either way. **Do not leave it answering `false`
  silently** — that is the trap B116 named and B125 confirmed.

## Do not assume it mirrors is_const

`is_const` turned out to be recoverable from the `decl` suffix. Access
specifiers may not appear in `decl` at all. **Measure before deciding**, and
report what the dumps actually contain rather than what would be convenient.

## While you are there

Audit the rest of the struct the same way. Two dead fields found by accident in
one night suggests the question "is this field ever populated?" has never been
asked systematically of `CoreMethod`. One pass over every field, with the counts
recorded in a test the way B125's 1002/301/352/26/0 counts now are, closes the
whole class of defect instead of the next instance of it.